vision.analysis:
The manifesto was unveiled in November 2026 as 'The Reset Agenda: A Governing Programme for a Productive, Secure, Capable and Accountable Federation'. It is organised around economic, security, education and socio-political resets, with principal commitments in years one to three and consolidation in year four (PROMISE). Quantified targets reported include:
- the federal deficit within the 3 per cent of GDP statutory ceiling by the end of year three;
- the debt-service-to-revenue ratio cut from 67.22 per cent (January-September 2025) to no more than 50 per cent;
- refinery decisions within 12 months;
- state police operating by the end of year three;
- Primary Three literacy at 50 per cent and numeracy at 65 per cent;
- a 100-day delivery plan.

The socio-political pillar proposes reviewing federal, state and local responsibilities, transferring functions together with funding, a greater state role in mining licences, and letting states reorganise local councils (PROMISE). Earlier, he framed the campaign as undoing what he called the 'hard policies' of the current administration. ANALYSIS: the manifesto is unusually specific on deadlines and accountability. It relies on redirecting existing spending rather than new funding. Many items, including state police, devolution and council reorganisation, need constitutional amendments that require National Assembly and state assembly majorities. The full document was not available for this review, so the summary relies on press coverage.
